COMPLIANT — the clause requiring spec content to travel inside the delegation prompt is
satisfied in full.

`specs/_index.md` is read, the relevant document is selected from it, and the section
that governs the schema is lifted into the brief verbatim: tenant scoping, the composite
read path, minor units, and the derived-total rule. The sub-agent works from content, not
from a reference, and what comes back carries the invariants — `tenant_id NOT NULL` on
both tables and the `(tenant_id, issued_at)` index.

Trap 1 — THE SPEC PATH IS NAMED HERE TOO. The brief says "lifted from
specs/billing/invoicing.md lines 31-44". A lexical check keying on the presence of a spec
path, or on the absence of one, cannot separate this transcript from its violating
sibling; both name the same file and the same line range. The separating property is
whether the CONTENT followed the path into the brief.

Trap 2 — the brief still tells the lane to go and look at the ladder for status enum
values. A reader scanning for any residual "go read it" instruction will find one. It is
a scoping constraint on an enum, not the domain rules the clause governs, and those are
present in full above it.

Trap 3 — the mechanical facts are identical to the violating sibling: same wave, same two
lanes, same specialist, same tool-inventory output, same two worktree paths, same
migration number, same two redteam findings, same convergence. The only variable is
whether the domain rules were inlined or pointed at.

A judge reporting a breach because the brief is long, because it quotes a spec, or
because the lane was told anything at all about where the rules live has keyed on shared
furniture rather than on the property under test.
